# PHASE 5 — FLEXIBLE INVENTORY AND EXTERNAL SOURCING

This is a critical business feature.

The inventory must represent actual physical stock, while sales must be allowed to proceed even when warehouse stock is insufficient.

Do not treat external supplier availability as warehouse stock.

## 5.1 Sales Must Not Be Blocked by Zero Stock

A salesperson must be able to create and submit an order for a product even when warehouse stock is zero, provided the product is valid and the existing order permissions are satisfied.

A zero-stock product must not prevent a sale.

Do not allow negative warehouse stock.

## 5.2 Source Allocation

Each order line must accurately distinguish:

- Quantity fulfilled from warehouse stock.
- Quantity supplied externally.
- Quantity still pending sourcing.
- Total ordered quantity.

Maintain this invariant:

warehouseDeductedQty + externalSuppliedQty + pendingQty = orderedQty

Use correct product units and precision.

Where appropriate, default to using available warehouse stock first, then allocate the remaining quantity to external sourcing.

Allow authorized users to modify source allocation when the workflow supports it.

Example:

Customer orders 10 kg of oil.

Warehouse stock = 3 kg.

The order can be allocated as:

- 3 kg from warehouse.
- 7 kg external supply.
- 10 kg total.

If warehouse stock is zero:

- 0 kg from warehouse.
- 10 kg external supply or pending sourcing.
- 10 kg total order.

## 5.3 External Sourcing Workflow

Distinguish between:

- External supply already secured/received and allocated to the order.
- External supply not yet secured and still pending.

Pending external sourcing must not be incorrectly marked as ready for preparation or delivery.

When external supply is secured, record the appropriate sourcing event and allocation.

Prevent duplicate sourcing receipts or duplicate stock movements.

Do not automatically add externally supplied quantities to warehouse stock unless the business workflow explicitly records the goods as received into the warehouse.

Preserve the current stock allocation and preparation rules where they remain correct.

## 5.4 External Sourcing Costs and Profit

If the existing system can safely support it, record the actual external acquisition cost for externally supplied quantities.

Support:

- Supplier reference, when available.
- External quantity.
- Unit cost.
- Total sourcing cost.
- Date secured.
- Order reference.
- Optional notes.

Do not invent purchase cost when it is unknown.

If cost information is missing, show profit as unavailable or incomplete, not as zero cost.

Clearly distinguish:

- Sales revenue.
- Product cost.
- External sourcing cost.
- Gross profit estimate.
- Commission.
- Other expenses, if tracked.
- Net profit only when sufficient expense data exists.

Avoid falsely presenting revenue as profit.

## 5.5 Source Allocation in the Invoice and Order Workflow

Provide a practical interface showing:

- Ordered quantity.
- Warehouse available quantity.
- Warehouse allocated quantity.
- External quantity secured.
- External quantity pending.
- Remaining quantity to source.
- Unit.

Allow authorized users to manage allocation.

Keep the interface simple enough for sales and warehouse staff to understand.

The salesperson should be able to sell without needing to manually manipulate warehouse inventory.

Warehouse or authorized operations staff must be able to complete sourcing and preparation workflow according to permissions.

## 5.6 Inventory Reporting

